== 10. Comparing Ourselves with Worse Men

The tenth #emph[device] that Satan has to draw the souls of men to sin is by working on them to be frequent in#footnote[#emph[Non vulnera sed munera ostendit.] He shows not his need, but his worth, and insists not only on his comparisons, but on his contrasts: “I am not like this tax collector.” They are very good, who are not very bad; and those who are very bad always value themselves above the market price.] comparing themselves and their ways with those who are reputed or reported to be worse than themselves. By this device the devil drew the proud Pharisee to bless himself in a cursed condition: “God, I thank You that I am not like the other men—swindlers, evildoers, adulterers—or even like this tax collector,” etc. “Why,” says Satan, “you swear only petty oaths, such as by your faith and truth, etc., but such and such people swear by wounds and blood. You are now and then a little wanton, but such and such people daily defile and pollute themselves by actual uncleanness and filthiness. You cheat and take advantage of your neighbors in things that are only toys and trifles, but such and such people cheat and take advantage of others in things of the greatest concern, even to their ruin and undoing. You only sit and chat and sip with the drunkard, but such and such people sit and drink themselves drunk with the drunkard. You are only a little proud in heart and dress, in looks and words,” etc.

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are as follows.]

The first #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider this: there is no greater or clearer argument to prove a man a#footnote[History speaks of a kind of witches who, going out, would put on their eyes, but returning home, boxed them up again. So do hypocrites.] hypocrite than to be sharp-sighted toward others and blind at home; #emph[than to see a speck in another man’s eye, and not a beam in his own eye;] than to use spectacles to look at other men’s sins, rather than mirrors to look at his own; than to be always holding his finger on other men’s sores; than to be amplifying and exaggerating other men’s sins, and lessening his own, etc.

The second #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to spend more time#footnote[The nearer we draw to God and his word, the more rottenness we will find in our bones. The more any man looks into the body of the sun, the less he sees when he looks down again. It is said of the #emph[basilisk] that if he looks into a mirror, he dies at once. So will sin and a sinner (in a spiritual sense) when the soul looks into the word, which is God’s mirror, etc.] comparing your#super[+] inward and outward actions with the rule, with that word by which you#super[+] must be judged at last, than comparing yourselves#super[+] with those who are worse than yourselves#super[+]. The man who, comparing himself with others who are worse than himself, may seem (to himself and others) to be an angel, may yet, comparing himself with the word, see himself to be like the devil, yes, a very devil: “Have I not chosen you#super[+], the Twelve? Yet one of you#super[+] is a devil!” Such men are as like him as if they were spit out of his mouth. Satan is called the god of this world because, as God at first only spoke the word and it was done, so if the devil only holds up his finger, or gives the least hint, they will do his will, though they ruin their souls forever. Ah, what monsters these men would appear to be, if only they compared themselves with a righteous rule, and not with the most unrighteous men! They would appear to be as black as hell itself.

The third #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is seriously to consider that though your sins are not as great as others’, yet without sound repentance on your side and pardoning mercy on God’s, you will be as certainly damned#footnote[As in heaven one is more glorious than another, so in hell one will be more miserable than another. Augustine.] as others, though not equally tormented with others. Though hell will not be so hot for you as for others, yet you will go to hell as certainly as others, unless the glorious grace of God shines on you in the face of Christ. God will suit men’s punishments to their sins. The greatest sins will be attended with the greatest punishments, and lesser sins with lesser punishments. Alas, what a poor comfort this will be to you when you come to die: to consider that you will not be equally tormented with others, and yet must be forever shut out from the glorious presence of God, Christ, angels and saints, and from those good things of eternal life that are so many that they exceed number,#footnote[The gate of favor, the gate of hope, the gate of mercy, the gate of glory, the gate of consolation and the gate of salvation will be forever shut against them, Matt 25:10.] so great that they exceed measure, so precious that they exceed all valuing! It is certain that the tears of hell are not enough to bewail the loss of heaven. The worm of grief gnaws as painfully as the fire burns. If those souls in Acts 20:37, 38 wept because they would see Paul’s face no more, how deplorable is the eternal loss of the beatific vision! But this is not all. You will not only be shut out of heaven, but shut up in hell forever; not only shut out from the presence of God and angels, etc., but shut up with devils and damned spirits forever; not only shut out from#footnote[It was a good saying of Chrysostom, speaking of hell (#emph[Ne quaeramus ubi sit, sed quomodo illam fugiamus]): let us not seek where it is, but how we may escape it.] those sweet, surpassing, inexpressible and everlasting pleasures that are at God’s right hand, but shut up forever under torments that are easeless, remediless and endless. Ah, souls! Would it not be ten thousand times better for you#super[+] to break off your#super[+] sins by repentance than to go on in your#super[+] sins until you#super[+] feel the truth of what you#super[+] now hear? The God of #emph[Israel] is very merciful. Ah, that you#super[+] would repent and return, so that your#super[+] souls might live forever! Remember this: grievous is the torment of the damned for the bitterness of the punishments, but most grievous for the eternity of the punishments. For to be tormented without end, this is#footnote[Surely one good means to escape hell is to take a turn or two in hell by our daily meditations.] what goes beyond all the bounds of despair. Ah, how the thought of this makes the damned roar and cry out in anguish of heart, and tear their hair, and gnash their teeth, and rage in madness, that they must dwell in everlasting burnings forever!
